module

public import SphereCeti.Lattice.IntegralPresentation
public import SphereCeti.ZLattice.Covolume.Bound

/-!
# Presented lattices have covolume at least one

A full lattice `Λ ⊆ ℝ^d` with an integral Gram presentation is integral, and an integral full
lattice has covolume at least one: its squared covolume is the absolute value of the Gram
determinant, a nonzero integer.  Combining `IntegralPresentation.isIntegral_real` with
`ZLattice.IsIntegral.one_le_covolume` gives the bound directly for presented lattices.

In the uniqueness argument of Cohn and Elkies, this bounds from below the covolume of the even
lattice generated by the translated centers of an optimal periodic packing, which comes with an
integral presentation.

## Main statements

* `SphereCeti.EuclideanLattice.IntegralPresentation.one_le_covolume`: a lattice with an integral
  presentation has covolume at least one.

## References

* H. Cohn and N. Elkies, *New upper bounds on sphere packings I*, Annals of Mathematics 157
  (2003), 689–714, Section 8.
-/

public section

namespace SphereCeti.EuclideanLattice.IntegralPresentation

/-- A full lattice in `ℝ^d` with an integral Gram presentation has covolume at least one. -/
theorem one_le_covolume {d : ℕ} {Λ : Submodule ℤ (EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin d))}
    [DiscreteTopology Λ] [IsZLattice ℝ Λ] (G : IntegralPresentation Λ) :
    1 ≤ ZLattice.covolume Λ :=
  G.isIntegral_real.one_le_covolume

end SphereCeti.EuclideanLattice.IntegralPresentation
